\title{cyRAVEN: Gated Multi-Sample Flow Cytometry Embedding and Differential Analysis}
\description{
\if{html}{\figure{logo.svg}{options: style='float: right' alt='logo' width='120'}}

An end-to-end workflow for batches of flow cytometry standard (FCS) files. Gating thresholds are derived from each sample's own marker density rather than transferred from fixed coordinates, populations are scored from a declarative specification held in YAML, and one shared 'UMAP' embedding is built per marker panel. Differential abundance and differential state are tested on per-sample summaries so that donors, not cells, are the replicates, following the aggregation strategy of Weber and others (2019) \doi{10.1038/s42003-019-0415-5}. Compositional constraints on population percentages are handled with the centred log-ratio, batch structure is quantified against a permutation null rather than silently corrected, and an unsupervised self-organising map clustering provides a cross-check that can contradict the gate specification.
}
\section{Overview}{

\code{cyRAVEN} takes a directory of FCS files and produces a gated, embedded,
statistically tested result set. The pipeline runs in nine stages, each of
which is also callable on its own:

\enumerate{
\item \strong{Read} -- \code{\link[=read_fcs_resolved]{read_fcs_resolved()}} resolves \verb{$PnN}/\verb{$PnS} to marker
symbols and applies the spillover matrix when the file carries one.
\item \strong{Transform} -- \code{\link[=derive_cofactor_pooled]{derive_cofactor_pooled()}} derives the arcsinh
cofactor from the data rather than assuming one.
\item \strong{Gate} -- \code{\link[=apply_gate_hierarchy]{apply_gate_hierarchy()}} derives scatter, singlet,
viability and CD45 gates per sample; \code{\link[=density_valley]{density_valley()}} places each
marker threshold at that sample's own bimodal split.
\item \strong{Score} -- \code{\link[=score_populations]{score_populations()}} evaluates a declarative population
specification (see the \code{config} vignette).
\item \strong{Embed} -- \code{\link[=run_umap]{run_umap()}} builds one shared embedding per marker
panel from a size-balanced subsample.
\item \strong{Test} -- \code{\link[=stats_group_comparison]{stats_group_comparison()}} for abundance,
\code{\link[=stats_marker_state]{stats_marker_state()}} for marker state, both on per-sample values.
\item \strong{Diagnose} -- \code{\link[=batch_mixing_report]{batch_mixing_report()}}, \code{\link[=stats_threshold_drift]{stats_threshold_drift()}},
\code{\link[=stats_confounding]{stats_confounding()}} and \code{\link[=cluster_gate_agreement]{cluster_gate_agreement()}}.
\item \strong{Explain} -- \code{\link[=explain_cluster]{explain_cluster()}} runs the gating stage backwards:
given cells the specification does not describe, it learns a two-marker
gating strategy that selects them. Descriptive only; it proposes gates
and never alters a scored population.
\item \strong{Report} -- figures and CSVs, plus \code{\link[=write_run_manifest]{write_run_manifest()}}.
}

\code{\link[=run_cyraven]{run_cyraven()}} runs all of it. \code{system.file("scripts", "cyraven.R", package = "cyRAVEN")} is a command-line front end to the same function.
}

\section{Statistical stance}{

Replicates are samples, never cells. Anything computed over pooled cells is
labelled descriptive and carries no p-value, because the number of cells is a
property of acquisition rather than of the design. See the
\code{vignette("statistics", package = "cyRAVEN")}.
}

